      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this certificate markdown carefully.\n\nThis appears to be pages 2 and 3 of an OIML Certificate of Conformity, but the OCR output is actually... a GLM-OCR output that looks like a ChatGPT-style summary of the certificate pages rather than the raw certificate text. Interesting. But my job is to extract the certificate data from this markdown.\n\nKey info:\n- OIML Certificate No.: R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07\n- Issuing Authority: FORCE Certification A/S\n- Date of Issue: 24 October 2023\n- Signed by: Jens Hovgård Jensen, Certification Manager\n\nScheme: R76/2006-A → Recommendation R76, edition 2006, scheme A.\nCertificate number: R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07\n\nAssociated reports:\n- Type examination report: 122-29322.10, 30 March 2023, 119 pages\n- Type evaluation report: 122-29322.90.20, 04 September 2023, 27 pages\n\nDocumentation file no. 122-29322\n\nRevision history: Initial version, 24 October 2023.\n\nCertificate type: This is a non-automatic weighing instrument (R76) — indicator/weighing module type designations: ADW-E / ADW-C / AXM / AGS / VGS. These look like weighing indicators/modules (A&D Instruments perhaps? ADW = weighing module... Actually ADW-E/ADW-C, AXM, AGS, VGS — these look like A&D weighing indicators). Category: likely \"Indicator\" or \"Weighing module\". Given R76 and the type names, these are probably weighing indicators (digital weigh modules). I'll categorize as \"Indicator\" — hmm, but I shouldn't fabricate. The certificate doesn't explicitly say \"indicator\". It says \"measuring instrument type\". Given types ADW-E, ADW-C, AXM, AGS, VGS with software IDs... These are A&D weighing indicators. Actually let me think: ADW (A&D Weighing?) — ADW-E, ADW-C are weighing modules/indicators. AXM is a weigh module. AGS and VGS... Hmm.\n\nThe safest: category — the certificate doesn't state it explicitly. But the schema requires category. R76 is \"Non-automatic weighing instruments\". The types listed with accuracy classes III and IIII, weighing ranges single/multi-interval/multi-range, Max 0.3 kg to 30 kg, verification scale intervals, tare, zero setting devices, RS232 interface... These are weighing instruments — indicators/modules. Given \"ADW\" = analog/digital weighing... I'll use \"Indicator\" since no weighing platform is described (no load cell mentioned; it has power supply, software, interfaces — typical indicator). Actually these might be \"weighing modules\" (ADW = A&D weighing). Hmm. AGS/VGS could be... A&D AD-4211B? Not sure.\n\nGiven uncertainty, I could set category to \"Indicator\" — the descriptive annex describes software, interfaces, zero setting devices — all indicator characteristics. There's no mention of a weighing platform/load receptor, so it's a modular indicator certification. I'll go with \"Indicator\".\n\nWait — but rule 8: don't fabricate. Category isn't explicitly stated. But the schema expects category; I'll use \"Indicator\" as best inference given R76 modular indicator context. Alternatively \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". Hmm. The schema examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\". For R76 weighing modules/indicators, \"Indicator\" fits best.\n\nModel family: The cert covers multiple models: ADW-E, ADW-C, AXM, AGS, VGS. Type designations: [\"ADW-E\", \"ADW-C\", \"AXM\", \"AGS\", \"VGS\"].\n\nCharacteristics:\n- Accuracy class: III and IIII → this is type_level: value \"III, IIII\" or list? It's not per-model — applies to all. Value could be string \"III and IIII\" or an array. Schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. I'll use string \"III, IIII\".\n- Weighing ranges: single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range → type_level: value string.\n- Maximum capacity (Max): 0.3 kg to 30 kg → {min: 0.3, max: 30}, unit \"kg\". This is a range across the family — could be model_level but we don't have per-model values. Keep as type_level range.\n- Minimum capacity (Min): 20 × e → formula string \"20 × e\".\n- Max verification scale intervals (n): ≤6000 (single interval); ≤2×5000 (multi-interval/multi-range) → this is config_level! Axis: \"interval_configuration\" with conditions \"single interval\" → 6000, \"multi-interval/multi-range\" → 2×5000. Value \"2×5000\" as string. Or max 5000? It says ≤2×5000, meaning up to 2 partial intervals of 5000 each. I'd record as string \"2×5000\".\n- Verification scale interval (e): ≥0.05 g → {min: 0.05, max: null}, unit \"g\".\n- Maximum tare effect: −Max → value \"−Max\" or \"-Max\" string. Hmm, \"−Max\" — negative of Max. Value: \"-Max\" string. Keep it as string.\n- Operating temperature range: −10 °C / +40 °C → {min: -10, max: 40}, unit \"°C\".\n- Power supply: 12VDC external (100–240VAC 50/60 Hz) or 6VDC rechargeable battery → split:\n  - external_power_supply_voltage: 12 V DC (value 12, unit \"V DC\" or \"V\")\n  - mains_supply_voltage: 100–240 V AC\n  - mains_supply_frequency: 50–60 Hz\n  - battery_voltage: 6 V DC (rechargeable)\n  \nPer rule 3: split. Let me do:\n- power_supply_type: \"12V DC external, 100–240VAC mains, or 6VDC rechargeable battery\"? Better to have separate characteristics:\n  - external_power_supply_voltage_dc: 12 V\n  - mains_power_supply_voltage_ac: {min:100, max:240} V\n  - mains_power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n  - battery_voltage: 6 V (rechargeable battery)\n\nSoftware identification (model_level!):\n- AXM & ADW-C: CE033\n- ADW-E: CE031 and CE032\n- AGS & VGS: CE034\n\nThis is attribute × model matrix: attribute \"software_identification\". Models: ADW-C, ADW-E, AXM, AGS, VGS. Values:\n- ADW-C: CE033\n- AXM: CE033\n- ADW-E: CE031 and CE032 → string \"CE031, CE032\"\n- AGS: CE034\n- VGS: CE034\n\nPermitted devices — description-ish info. Could go into type_level characteristics or description. Options:\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {min: null, max: 20}, unit \"%\" with reference Max → value \"≤20% Max\". Hmm. I could record:\n  - initial_zero_setting_range: 20% of Max → value: {min:null, max:20}, unit_symbol \"%\", value reference Max. The structured value shape... value 20, unit \"%\" — reference Max lost. Could add separate char \"zero_setting_reference\": \"Max\". Or keep the description field.\n  \nMaybe better to put permitted devices into `certified_type.description` free-text, and extract the numeric ones:\n- initial_zero_setting_range: max 20% Max\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: max 4% Max\n- zero_tracking_range: max 4% Max\n\nI'll include those as config/type-level with value {min:null,max:20}, unit \"%\", and maybe note reference in a companion... The schema value shape allows string. Rule 2 says formulas/symbols keep as string. \"≤20% Max\" — I could do value: {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol: \"%\", and attribute name includes reference? Hmm, no separate field for reference. I'll just record with the \"%\" unit and rely on attribute naming... Actually maybe cleaner: value: 20, unit \"%\", attribute \"initial_zero_setting_range_max_max\"... too convoluted. \n\nI'll extract them into type_level with attribute names like `initial_zero_setting_range` value {min:null, max:20}, unit_symbol \"%\", plus add a footnote? No. Alternatively record as string \"≤20% Max\" — but rule says numbers not strings, unit separation. The reference \"Max\" is like \"150% of Emax\" example: value 150, unit \"%\", separate characteristic `safe_overload_reference` style. I could add `zero_setting_reference: \"Max\"` — a bit noisy but acceptable. Actually rule 3 example says \"(Or omit if too noisy.)\" — I'll include the numeric extraction and put full detail in description.\n\nIndicators: stable equilibrium, zero, net indicators — description.\n\nInterfaces: RS232 — not secured. Record as type_level characteristic: interface: \"RS232 (unsecured)\". Or config-level. I'll do `interfaces: {value: \"RS232\", ...}` hmm. Maybe:\n- type_level: \"interface_rs232_secured\": false? Don't fabricate booleans... The text says \"the interface does not have to be secured\". I'll put in description.\n\nCertificate-level:\n- number: \"R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not present. The report numbers 122-29322 are FORCE project numbers; \"documentation file no. 122-29322\". I could put project_number \"122-29322\"? It's the documentation file number. Not exactly project number. Leave out or include as documentation file in test_reports role \"documentation_file\" with id 122-29322. Yes, test_reports role documentation_file.\n- page_total: The certificate — page 2 of 3 mentioned. So 3 pages. Hmm, \"page 2 of 3\". I could set page_total: 3. That's from the summary header \"page 2 of 3\". Reasonably safe.\n- member_state: \"DK\" (DK2 in cert number; FORCE is Denmark). Issuing authority Denmark — member_state: \"Denmark\"? The cert number includes DK2. I'll use \"DK\" or \"Denmark\". OIML issuer id: FORCE is DK-2? Actually OIML issuer IDs: NMi = 012? FORCE = DK-2? I shouldn't fabricate. member_state: \"Denmark\" — reasonable inference from DK. Hmm rule 8 don't fabricate... DK is in the number; FORCE Certification A/S is Danish. I'll put \"Denmark\".\n- date_issued: \"2023-10-24\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present; omit.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: FORCE Certification A/S\n- address_lines: not present — omit.\n- person_responsible: Jens Hovgård Jensen\n- person_title: Certification Manager\n- phone, email, website: omit.\n\nApplicants/Manufacturers: not present — omit. Hmm, page 1 (not provided) would have them. Omit.\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R76\"\n- edition: 2006 (the cert number R76/2006 → edition 2006). edition is int per schema. 2006.\n- amendment: null/omit\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [\"III\", \"IIII\"]\n\nTest reports:\n- {id: \"122-29322.10\", date: \"2023-03-30\", pages: 119, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — it's \"Type examination report\". role options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. Type examination report → \"type_evaluation_report\"? There's also \"Type evaluation report\" 122-29322.90.20. Hmm. \"Type examination report\" 122-29322.10, 119 pages — that's the examination/test report. The \"Type evaluation report\" 122-29322.90.20, 27 pages — evaluation_report. I'll map:\n  - 122-29322.10 → role \"test_report\"? or \"type_evaluation_report\"? The schema roles: type_evaluation_report, test_report, documentation_file, evaluation_report. \"Type examination report\" is closest to \"type_evaluation_report\"... but then the actual \"Type evaluation report\" would be \"evaluation_report\". Hmm. Alternatively examination → test_report, evaluation → evaluation_report. I think:\n  - Type examination report 122-29322.10 → role \"type_evaluation_report\" (it's the main report)... \n\nLet me think again. In OIML CS, the \"test report\" contains test results; the \"type evaluation report\" is the summary. Here \"Type examination report\" (119 pages) = detailed test report → role \"test_report\". \"Type evaluation report\" (27 pages) → role \"type_evaluation_report\"? or \"evaluation_report\". The schema has both \"type_evaluation_report\" and \"evaluation_report\". I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for the Type evaluation report and \"test_report\" for the Type examination report. Documentation file 122-29322 → role \"documentation_file\", pages: null.\n\nRevision history:\n- revision: \"Initial version\" — schema says revision string e.g. \"0\". The text says \"Initial version | 24 October 2023 | –\". So revision: \"Initial version\", date: \"2023-10-24\", changes: null/omit or \"–\". I'll omit changes (dash is placeholder) or set \"Initial version\". Just revision \"Initial version\", date.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nModel family: models: ADW-E, ADW-C, AXM, AGS, VGS — 5 variants. model_id each.\n\nModel-level characteristics:\n- software_identification: per model as above.\n\nShould Max etc. be model_level? We only know the range 0.3–30 kg across all models, not per-model. Keep type_level as range.\n\nConfig-level:\n- max_verification_scale_intervals: axis \"weighing_range_type\": single interval → 6000; multi-interval/multi-range → \"2×5000\". Values: {condition: \"single interval\", value: {min:null, max:6000}}? The structured form per rule 5: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`. value 6000, unit null. For \"2×5000\" value string \"2×5000\".\n\nHmm, ≤6000 → value could be {min: null, max: 6000}. But config_level value shape — keep consistent: value: {min: null, max: 6000}, unit_symbol: null. And {condition: \"multi-interval or multi-range\", value: \"2×5000\", unit_symbol: null}.\n\nPower supply: is it per-model? Not specified per model; type_level.\n\nActually wait — should power supply variants be config_level? \"12VDC external (100–240VAC 50/60 Hz) or 6VDC rechargeable battery\" — two power supply options across the family. Not clear per-model. Keep type_level split chars:\n- external_dc_supply_voltage: 12, \"V\"\n- mains_ac_supply_voltage: {100,240}, \"V\"\n- mains_supply_frequency: {50,60}, \"Hz\"\n- battery_supply_voltage: 6, \"V\" — battery rechargeable → separate char `battery_type: \"rechargeable\"`? Might be too noisy; I'll add note in description.\n\nAccuracy class: \"III and IIII\" → accuracy_classes in recommendation, and maybe type_level characteristic `accuracy_class: \"III, IIII\"`. I'll include in recommendation only; maybe also type_level. The schema has accuracy_classes under recommendation. Fine.\n\nWeighing ranges: type_level characteristic `weighing_ranges: \"single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range\"` — value string.\n\nMaximum tare effect: −Max → value \"-Max\" string, unit null.\n\nType description: free text — build from permitted devices etc.:\n\"Weighing modules/indicators for non-automatic weighing instruments; permitted devices: initial zero setting (≤20% Max), semi-automatic zero setting (≤4% Max), zero tracking (≤4% Max), semi-automatic and automatic subtractive tare devices, printing/totalization/unit toggling devices (g and kg only), sealed gravity compensation device; stable equilibrium, zero and net indicators; RS232 interface (not required to be secured).\"\n\nModule designation: null. Components: none named. matrix_tables: none (software ID table is small; could be matrix_tables? It's attribute × model, better as model_level).\n\nCharacteristics type_level:\n1. weighing_ranges: value string\n2. maximum_capacity: {min 0.3, max 30}, kg\n3. minimum_capacity: \"20 × e\" → \"20 × e\" — avoid special char issues? \"×\" is fine in JSON. Maybe \"20 × e\" or \"20 x e\". Keep \"20 × e\".\n4. verification_scale_interval: {min 0.05, max null}, g\n5. maximum_tare_effect: \"-Max\"\n6. operating_temperature_range: {min -10, max 40}, °C\n7. initial_zero_setting_range: {min null, max 20}, \"%\", reference Max → add zero_setting_reference? I'll skip separate reference chars, description covers it. Hmm, but rule 3 example shows separate characteristic for reference. For cleanliness I'll include:\n   - initial_zero_setting_range: {max:20} %\n   - semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: {max:4} %\n   - zero_tracking_range: {max:4} %\n8. power: as above four chars.\n9. interfaces: value \"RS232\" — maybe `interface_types: \"RS232\"` and `interface_security_required: false`? Fabrication-ish. The statement \"does not have to be secured\" is a note. I'll add `interface_rs232_security: \"not required to be secured\"`? Hmm. I'll do interfaces: {value: \"RS232\", unit: null} and put the security note in description.\n\nModel_level software ID:\nattribute: \"software_identification\", unit null, values:\n- {model: \"ADW-C\", value: \"CE033\"}\n- {model: \"AXM\", value: \"CE033\"}\n- {model: \"ADW-E\", value: \"CE031 and CE032\"} → maybe \"CE031, CE032\"\n- {model: \"AGS\", value: \"CE034\"}\n- {model: \"VGS\", value: \"CE034\"}\n\nCertificate number parse: R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07. number full string. scheme \"A\".\n\npage_total: 3.\n\ndate_issued \"2023-10-24\".\n\nCategory: I'm torn between \"Indicator\" and \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". Given R76 and modular indicators (A&D ADW-E etc. are actually weighing modules — ADW = \"A&D Weighing\"), category \"Indicator\" seems acceptable given the schema examples. Actually, hmm, AXM could be \"AD-8121\"? Not sure. The descriptive annex mentions software, interfaces, zero setting, tare — indicator/module features. I'll use \"Indicator\".\n\nActually wait — could AGS/VGS be platform scales? AGS — A&D... VGS... Not sure. There's no load cell or platform description. Software ID per model, RS232 — modules. \"Indicator\" it is.\n\nType designations: all five. Should \"Type\" values with slashes be split: \"ADW-E / ADW-C / AXM / AGS / VGS\" → [\"ADW-E\", \"ADW-C\", \"AXM\", \"AGS\", \"VGS\"]. Yes.\n\nNow the JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 3,\n    \"member_state\": \"Denmark\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2023-10-24\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"FORCE Certification A/S\",\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Jens Hovgård Jensen\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Certification Manager\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Indicator\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"ADW-E\", \"ADW-C\", \"AXM\", \"AGS\", \"VGS\"],\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"ADW-E\", \"label\": \"ADW-E\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"ADW-C\", \"label\": \"ADW-C\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"AXM\", \"label\": \"AXM\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"AGS\", \"label\": \"AGS\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"VGS\", \"label\": \"VGS\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"III and IIII\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      ...\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"software_identification\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [...]}\n    ],\n    \"config_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"max_verification_scale_intervals\", \"axis\": \"weighing range type\", \"values\": [\n        {\"condition\": \"single interval\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 6000}, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n        {\"condition\": \"multi-interval or multi-range\", \"value\": \"2 × 5000\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n      ]}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...],\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nShould I include empty arrays like footnotes: []? Schema lists footnotes[]; omit if none or include empty. I'll omit empty ones except maybe include. I'll include footnotes as empty array? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit.\n\nAccuracy class value: \"III, IIII\" — maybe just put in recommendation and omit from characteristics to avoid duplication? It's a characteristic of the type. I'll include both.\n\nFor unit_id: all null unless certain. \"kg\", \"g\", \"°C\", \"V\", \"Hz\", \"%\" — I don't know exact unitsdb IDs, keep null.\n\nStructured values — each value object shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. Include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] consistently.\n\nMaximum capacity across family: 0.3 to 30 kg. But wait — \"0.3 kg to 30 kg\" — a range. {min: 0.3, max: 30}.\n\nPower supply splits:\n- external_dc_supply_voltage: value 12, unit \"V\"\n- mains_ac_supply_voltage: {100,240}, \"V\"\n- mains_supply_frequency: {50,60}, \"Hz\"\n- battery_supply_voltage: 6, \"V\"\n\nAttribute naming canonical: \"external_power_supply_voltage\", \"mains_power_supply_voltage\", \"mains_power_supply_frequency\", \"battery_supply_voltage\".\n\nZero/tare chars:\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {min:null,max:20}, \"%\"\n- semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: {min:null,max:4}, \"%\"\n- zero_tracking_range: {min:null,max:4}, \"%\"\n\nReference Max — maybe include `zero_setting_reference: \"Max\"`? The description covers it. Skip.\n\nWeighing ranges: attribute \"weighing_range_types\", value \"single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range\".\n\nMaximum tare effect: \"-Max\" — use \"-Max\" (hyphen-minus). value: \"-Max\".\n\nInterfaces: \"interface_types\": \"RS232\". Security note in description.\n\nDescription text: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument modules/indicators for single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range applications. Permitted devices: initial zero setting (≤20% Max), semi-automatic zero setting (≤4% Max), zero tracking (≤4% Max), semi-automatic and automatic subtractive tare, printing and totalization devices, unit toggling (g and kg only), sealed gravity compensation device; stable equilibrium, zero and net indicators. RS232 interface (does not have to be secured).\"\n\nTest report dates: 2023-03-30, 2023-09-04.\n\ntest_reports:\n[\n {\"id\": \"122-29322.10\", \"date\": \"2023-03-30\", \"pages\": 119, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"122-29322.90.20\", \"date\": \"2023-09-04\", \"pages\": 27, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"122-29322\", \"date\": null, \"pages\": null, \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n\nHmm \"Type examination report\" → maybe role \"type_evaluation_report\"? The two roles listed: type_evaluation_report vs test_report vs evaluation_report. \"Type examination report\" — I think mapping to \"test_report\" is reasonable since it's the 119-page test report. Actually in OIML CS terminology, the \"test report\" is per recommendation; \"type evaluation report\" is the overall. Here \"Type examination report\" = the tests; role \"test_report\". OK.\n\nRevision history:\n[{\"revision\": \"Initial version\", \"date\": \"2023-10-24\", \"changes\": null}]\nSchema: revision string e.g. \"0\". \"Initial version\" is fine. changes: the table says \"–\" — omit or null. I'll set changes: null or omit. Include \"changes\": null? Rule: omit if not present; a dash means no description. I'll omit.\n\nproject_number: The FORCE project number might be \"122-29322\" (report numbering base). Not explicitly \"project number\". Omit.\n\nmember_state: \"Denmark\" — from DK. Alternatively omit? The cert number has DK2 which denotes Denmark issuing authority #2. I'll include \"Denmark\".\n\nRecommendation: {\"id\": \"R76\", \"edition\": 2006, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [\"III\", \"IIII\"]}\n\nAmendment: omit (none indicated).\n\nOutput single JSON object, no fences.",
